The '''2024 UEFA Champions League final''' was the final match of the [[2023–24 UEFA Champions League]], the 69th season of Europe's premier club football tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 32nd season since it was renamed from the [[European Champion Clubs' Cup]] to the [[UEFA Champions League]]. It was held at [[Wembley Stadium]] in [[London]], England, on 1 June 2024,<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://editorial.uefa.com/resources/0279-163596ae271e-e04faa20e08e-1000/20220926_circular_2022_65_en.zip |title=International match calendar and access list for the 2023/24 season |number=65/2022 |magazine=UEFA Circular Letter |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=26 September 2022 |access-date=27 September 2022 |archive-date=21 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221021224010/https://editorial.uefa.com/resources/0279-163596ae271e-e04faa20e08e-1000/20220926_circular_2022_65_en.zip |url-status=live}}</ref> between German club [[Borussia Dortmund]] and Spanish club [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]]. Due to the postponement and relocation of the [[2020 UEFA Champions League final|2020 final]], the final hosts were shifted back a year, with London instead hosting the 2024 final.<ref name="ExCo 17 June">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/025e-0fb6101cfdbf-8b878372d79d-1000--uefa-competitions-to-resume-in-august/ |title=UEFA competitions to resume in August |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=17 June 2020 |access-date=17 June 2020 |archive-date=25 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200825183219/https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/about-uefa/news/025e-0fb6101cfdbf-8b878372d79d-1000--uefa-competitions-to-resume-in-august/?referrer=%2finsideuefa%2fnews%2fnewsid%3d2642232 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Real Madrid won the match 2–0 for [[European Cup and UEFA Champions League records and statistics#General performances|a record-extending 15th title]], and their sixth in eleven seasons.<ref name="summary">{{cite web |last=Pettit |first=Mark |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/028e-1b07e18d875a-ba78e4b9d9fc-1000/ |title=Real Madrid win Champions League: Carvajal and Vinícius Júnior see off Dortmund |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=1 June 2024 |access-date=2 June 2024 |archive-date=2 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240602071515/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/028e-1b07e18d875a-ba78e4b9d9fc-1000/ |url-status=live}}</ref> As winners, they earned a spot to play in the [[2024 UEFA Super Cup]], against the winners of the [[2023–24 UEFA Europa League]], [[Atalanta BC|Atalanta]]. They also earned a chance to compete in the [[2024 FIFA Intercontinental Cup|inaugural edition]] of the [[FIFA Intercontinental Cup]]. As they had already qualified for the [[2025 FIFA Club World Cup]] by winning the same tournament in 2022, the spot intended for the winners was redistributed via the UEFA club ranking, with Dortmund claiming one of those spots.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=FIFA Council confirms key details for FIFA Club World Cup 2025™ |url=https://www.fifa.com/fifaplus/en/tournaments/mens/fifa-club-world-cup/articles/fifa-council-confirms-key-details-club-world-cup-2025 |access-date=16 March 2024 |website=FIFA.com|publisher=FIFA}}</ref>

==Background==
For [[Borussia Dortmund]], this was their third UEFA Champions League final appearance, the first since the 1–2 [[Der Klassiker]] loss against [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] in [[2013 UEFA Champions League final|2013]], which was also held at [[Wembley Stadium]], and the first European final for manager [[Edin Terzić (footballer)|Edin Terzić]].<ref name="RSSSF">{{cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec1.html |title=European Champions' Cup/Champions League |publisher=[[RSSSF]] |date=27 January 2023 |access-date=10 June 2023 |archive-date=6 July 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090706013641/http://www.rsssf.com/tablese/ec1.html |url-status=live}}</ref> Additionally, Dortmund played one [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|European Cup Winners' Cup]] final (winning in [[1966 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1966]])<ref name="CWC">{{cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec2.html |title=European Cup Winners' Cup |publisher=[[RSSSF]] |date=26 January 2000 |access-date=2 June 2024 |archive-date=15 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230315030508/https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec2.html |url-status=live}}</ref> and two [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] finals (losing in [[1993 UEFA Cup final|1993]] and [[2002 UEFA Cup final|2002]]).<ref name="UCEL">{{cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec3b.html |title=UEFA Cup/Europa League |publisher=[[RSSSF]] |date=1 June 2023 |access-date=2 June 2024 |archive-date=3 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303191321/http://www.rsssf.com/tablese/ec3b.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

[[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] played in a record-extending 18th European Cup/UEFA Champions League final, and their second in three years. They previously won 14 finals (in [[1956 European Cup final|1956]], [[1957 European Cup final|1957]], [[1958 European Cup final|1958]], [[1959 European Cup final|1959]], [[1960 European Cup final|1960]], [[1966 European Cup final|1966]], [[1998 UEFA Champions League final|1998]], [[2000 UEFA Champions League final|2000]], [[2002 UEFA Champions League final|2002]], [[2014 UEFA Champions League final|2014]], [[2016 UEFA Champions League final|2016]], [[2017 UEFA Champions League final|2017]], [[2018 UEFA Champions League final|2018]] and [[2022 UEFA Champions League final|2022]]) and lost three ([[1962 European Cup final|1962]], [[1964 European Cup final|1964]] and [[1981 European Cup final|1981]]).<ref name="RSSSF"/> Their manager [[Carlo Ancelotti]] reached a record-extending sixth UEFA Champions League final as manager, winning in [[2003 UEFA Champions League final|2003]] and [[2007 UEFA Champions League final|2007]] and losing in [[2005 UEFA Champions League final|2005]] while in charge of [[AC Milan|Milan]], and winning the 2014 and 2022 finals with Real Madrid.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0275-15407684c988-5b733e851bf5-1000/ |title=Champions League-winning coaches: Carlo Ancelotti leads the way |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=1 June 2024 |access-date=2 June 2024 |archive-date=12 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231212061605/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0275-15407684c988-5b733e851bf5-1000/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Real Madrid also played in two European Cup Winners' Cup finals (losing in [[1971 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1971]] and [[1983 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1983]])<ref name="CWC"/> and two UEFA Cup finals (winning in [[1985 UEFA Cup final|1985]] and [[1986 UEFA Cup final|1986]]).<ref name="UCEL"/>

This was the first Champions League final between the two clubs,<ref name="RSSSF"/> and their first meeting in European competitions since the [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League group stage|2017–18 Champions League group stage]] encounters, when Madrid won [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League group stage#Borussia Dortmund v Real Madrid|3–1 away]] and [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League group stage#Real Madrid v Borussia Dortmund|3–2 at home]]. Of their previous fourteen encounters, Dortmund won three matches, Real won six matches and five finished as a draw.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.worldfootball.net/teams/te258/borussia-dortmund/records-all-time-appearances/ |title=Borussia Dortmund » Record against Real Madrid |date=2 June 2024 |work=WorldFootball.net |access-date=2 June 2024 |archive-date=2 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240602070120/https://www.worldfootball.net/teams/borussia-dortmund/real-madrid/11/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

Until the semi-finals, Borussia Dortmund had the best defence in the 2023–24 Champions League with six clean sheets and eight goals conceded. In contrast, Real Madrid goalkeepers had the most goal-scoring opportunities to prevent in the same season.<ref name=":02">{{cite web |date=1 June 2024 |access-date=1 June 2024 |language=de |title=CL-Finale gegen den BVB: Das sind die Gefahren für Real Madrid |website=neunzigplus.de |url=https://neunzigplus.de/champions-league/cl-finale-gefahren-fuer-real/ |archive-date=1 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240601170539/https://neunzigplus.de/champions-league/cl-finale-gefahren-fuer-real/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

