==Venue==
[[File:Wembley Stadium interior.jpg|thumb|275px|[[Wembley Stadium]] in [[London]] hosted the final.]]

This was the third UEFA Champions League final to take place at the rebuilt Wembley Stadium, having previously been held in [[2011 UEFA Champions League final|2011]] and 2013. Overall, it was the eighth final to be held in London, with the other five matches taking place at the [[Wembley Stadium (1923)|original Wembley Stadium]] in [[1963 European Cup final|1963]], [[1968 European Cup final|1968]], [[1971 European Cup final|1971]], [[1978 European Cup final|1978]], and [[1992 European Cup final|1992]]. The match was the ninth European Cup final held in England, with the 2003 final having been held at [[Old Trafford]] in [[Manchester]], equalling the record of nine European Cup finals held in each of Italy, Germany and Spain. It was also the thirteenth held in the United Kingdom, with the 1960, [[1976 European Cup final|1976]] and 2002 finals held in Scotland and the 2017 final held in Wales.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/EuroExperience/competitions/Publications/01/99/48/32/1994832_DOWNLOAD.pdf |title=UEFA Champions League Statistics Handbook 2013/14: Finals |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |year=2014 |access-date=24 September 2019 |archive-date=16 July 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210716203913/https://www.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/EuroExperience/competitions/Publications/01/99/48/32/1994832_DOWNLOAD.pdf |url-status=dead}}</ref> Wembley Stadium was also a host venue at [[UEFA Euro 2020]], with eight matches played at the stadium including the semi-finals and [[UEFA Euro 2020 final|final]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuro/history/news/0256-0dd74d2cf7cc-56813840afe0-1000/ |title=EURO 2020: host cities and stadiums |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=23 April 2021 |access-date=2 June 2024 |archive-date=2 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240602070219/https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuro/history/news/0256-0dd74d2cf7cc-56813840afe0-1000/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Host selection===
An open bidding process was launched on 22 February 2019 by UEFA to select the 2022 and 2023 UEFA Champions League final venues.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/024e-0f8e68aa43f5-6b3adef08395-1000--9-associations-bidding-to-host-2021-club-finals/ |title=9 associations bidding to host 2021 club finals |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=22 February 2019 |access-date=14 September 2019 |archive-date=18 June 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200618165213/https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/about-uefa/news/newsid=2593491.html |url-status=live}}</ref> Associations had until 22 March 2019 to express interest, and bid dossiers had to be submitted by 1 July 2019.<ref name="AP">{{cite news |url=https://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-soccer-champions-final/london-mayor-backs-bid-to-host-2023-champions-league-final-at-wembley-idUKKCN1UQ26G |title=London Mayor backs bid to host 2023 Champions League final at Wembley |website=[[Reuters]] |date=31 July 2019 |access-date=14 September 2019 |archive-date=19 September 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200919145455/https://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-soccer-champions-final/london-mayor-backs-bid-to-host-2023-champions-league-final-at-wembley-idUKKCN1UQ26G |url-status=dead}}</ref>

[[The Football Association]] was reported to have bid with Wembley Stadium in London to host the 2023 final, in order to mark the centenary of the opening of the original stadium in 1923.<ref name="AP"/> Wembley Stadium was selected by the UEFA Executive Committee during their meeting in [[Ljubljana]], Slovenia, on 24 September 2019,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0255-0e99f55f1d05-6273cba59e7c-1000--champions-league-final-hosts-for-2021-2022-2023-and-2024/ |title=Champions League final hosts announced for 2021, 2022 and 2023 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=24 September 2019 |access-date=24 September 2019 |archive-date=22 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191222151850/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2625232.html |url-status=live}}</ref> where the hosts for the [[2021 UEFA Champions League final|2021]] and 2022 UEFA Champions League finals were also appointed.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0255-0f8e6f9636b1-db5308c8e2e5-1000--uefa-executive-committee-agenda-for-ljubljana-meeting/ |title=UEFA Executive Committee agenda for Ljubljana meeting |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=17 September 2019 |access-date=17 September 2019 |archive-date=18 September 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190918021511/https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/about-uefa/news/newsid=2623092.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 17 June 2020, the UEFA Executive Committee announced that due to the postponement and relocation of the [[2020 UEFA Champions League final|2020 final]], London would instead host the 2024 final.<ref name="ExCo 17 June"/>

