Borussia Dortmund qualified for the Champions League group stage by finishing as runners-up in the [[2022–23 Bundesliga]]. In the group stage, they were drawn into Group F, alongside [[2022–23 Ligue 1|Ligue 1]] winners [[Paris Saint-Germain FC|Paris Saint-Germain]], [[2022–23 Serie A|Serie A]] fourth-place team Milan and [[2022–23 Premier League|Premier League]] fourth-place side [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]],<ref name="GS draw">{{cite web |title=UEFA Champions League group stage draw |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/draws/2024/2001775/ |date=31 August 2023 |access-date=8 May 2024 |url-status=live |archive-date=30 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230830141356/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/draws/2024/2001775/ }}</ref> which was widely regarded as the '[[group of death]]'.<ref>{{cite web |title=Hummels seals Champions League final place for Dortmund as PSG crash out |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/article/2024/may/07/paris-saint-germain-borussia-dortmund-champions-league-match-report |date=7 May 2024 |access-date=8 May 2024 |work=The Guardian |url-status=live |archive-date=10 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240510142137/https://www.theguardian.com/football/article/2024/may/07/paris-saint-germain-borussia-dortmund-champions-league-match-report}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title='Group of death' delivers as PSG, Newcastle and Milan all stay in hunt |url=https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/group-death-delivers-psg-newcastle-milan-all-stay-hunt-2023-11-29/ |date=29 November 2023 |access-date=8 May 2024 |publisher=Reuters |url-status=live |archive-date=29 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231129070716/https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/group-death-delivers-psg-newcastle-milan-all-stay-hunt-2023-11-29/}}</ref>

In Dortmund's opener of the group stage, they faced Paris Saint-Germain at the [[Parc des Princes]] and lost 0–2, with goals from [[Kylian Mbappé]] and [[Achraf Hakimi]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Paris-Dortmund |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039188/ |access-date=8 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=29 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230929225042/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039188/ }}</ref> On matchday 2, Dortmund drew in a 0–0 stalemate at the [[Westfalenstadion]] against Milan.<ref>{{cite web |title=Dortmund-Milan |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039109/ |access-date=8 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=29 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230929225928/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039109/ }}</ref> On matchday 3, Dortmund defeated Newcastle United away from home 1–0, with a goal from [[Felix Nmecha]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Newcastle-Dortmund |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039126/ |access-date=8 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=10 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240510142405/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039126/ }}</ref> On matchday 4, Dortmund won 2–0 over Newcastle United at home, with goals coming from [[Niclas Füllkrug]] and [[Julian Brandt]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Dortmund-Newcastle |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039130/ |access-date=8 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=10 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240510142337/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039130/ }}</ref> On matchday 5, Dortmund got a 3–1 win against Milan at the [[San Siro]], with goals by [[Marco Reus]], [[Jamie Bynoe-Gittens]] and [[Karim Adeyemi]], as [[Samuel Chukwueze]] got the temporary equaliser for the hosts.<ref>{{cite web |title=Milan-Dortmund |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039150/ |access-date=8 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=29 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230929223658/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039150/ }}</ref> On matchday 6, returning to the Westfalenstadion, the hosts held Paris Saint-Germain to a 1–1 draw, with a goal from Adeyemi and a strike from [[Warren Zaïre-Emery]] for the visitors.<ref>{{cite web |title=Dortmund-Paris |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039174/ |access-date=8 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=29 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230929225925/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039174/ }}</ref>

In the round of 16, Dortmund were drawn against Dutch club [[PSV Eindhoven]]. In the first leg held at the [[Philips Stadion]], Dortmund drew 1–1, with goals from [[Donyell Malen]] and [[Luuk de Jong]].<ref>{{cite web |title=PSV-Dortmund |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039919/ |access-date=8 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=18 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231218163319/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039919/ }}</ref> In the reverse leg, Dortmund defeated PSV 2–0, with goals from [[Jadon Sancho]] and Reus, to win 3–1 on aggregate and advance to the quarter-finals.<ref>{{cite web |title=Dortmund-PSV |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039929/ |access-date=8 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=13 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313065028/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039929/ }}</ref>

In the quarter-finals, Dortmund were drawn against Spanish side [[Atlético Madrid]]. In the first leg, at the [[Metropolitano Stadium]], the Germans suffered a 1–2 loss, with goals being scored by [[Rodrigo De Paul]], [[Samuel Lino]] and [[Sébastien Haller]], whose late goal rescued Dortmund's chances for the progression.<ref>{{cite web |title=Atlético de Madrid-Dortmund |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040493/ |access-date=8 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=15 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240315172842/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040493/ }}</ref> In the second leg, Dortmund produced a 4–2 win at home, trailing down 3–4 on aggregate in the second half and qualifying to the semi-finals 5–4 on aggregate, as Brandt, [[Ian Maatsen]], Füllkrug, [[Marcel Sabitzer]], [[Mats Hummels]] (own goal) and [[Ángel Correa]] got on the scoresheet.<ref>{{cite web |title=Dortmund-Atlético de Madrid |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040499/ |access-date=8 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=15 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240315172849/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040499/ }}</ref>

In the semi-finals, Dortmund were drawn against Paris Saint-Germain, making it a rematch of this season's group stage clash. In the first leg, at the Westfalenstadion, a lone winner from Füllkrug gave Dortmund a 1–0 victory.<ref>{{cite web |title=Dortmund-Paris |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040501/ |access-date=8 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=10 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240510142145/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040501/ }}</ref> In the second leg, at the Parc des Princes, the visitors won 1–0 once again, with the only goal of the match coming from Hummels. Dortmund won 2–0 on aggregate to qualify for their first Champions League final in eleven years.<ref>{{cite web |title=Paris-Dortmund |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040502/ |access-date=8 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=10 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240510142059/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040502/ }}</ref>

===Real Madrid===
{{see also|2023–24 Real Madrid CF season}}
{{multiple image
| align             = right
| direction         = horizontal
| total_width       = 440
| image1            = BHA 5 v Espanyol 1 pre season 30 07 2022 15 (cropped).jpg
| image2            = Rodrygo 2023 (cropped).jpg
| image3            = Vinicius Jr 2021.jpg
| footer            = Forward [[Joselu]] (left), who was on loan to [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] from [[RCD Espanyol|Espanyol]], scored two late decisive goals in the second leg of the semi-final against [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] to send Madrid to the final. He and fellow forwards [[Rodrygo]] (middle) and [[Vinícius Júnior]] (right) were their club's top scorers in the competition with five goals each.
}}

[[File:CSKA-RM18 (7) (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright=.8|The 2024 UEFA Champions League final marked [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] midfielder [[Toni Kroos]]' last club football match and his sixth appearance in the competition's final.]] 

Real Madrid qualified for the Champions League group stage as [[2022–23 La Liga]] runners-up. They were drawn in Group C, alongside reigning Serie A champions [[SSC Napoli|Napoli]], third placed [[2022–23 Primeira Liga|Primeira Liga]] team [[S.C. Braga|Braga]], and fourth placed Bundesliga team [[1. FC Union Berlin|Union Berlin]].<ref name="GS draw"/>

Madrid went on to end the group stage recording an impressive 6 out of 6 wins in all games. The team opened the group stage at the [[Santiago Bernabéu Stadium|Santiago Bernabéu]] against Union Berlin, with a late [[Jude Bellingham]] goal sealing a 1–0 win.<ref>{{cite web |title=Real Madrid-Union Berlin |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039183/ |access-date=9 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=22 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230922003656/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039183/ }}</ref> On matchday 2, the team grabbed a 3–2 away win against Napoli at the [[Stadio Diego Armando Maradona]], with finishes coming from [[Vinícius Júnior]], Bellingham, and an own goal from Napoli goalkeeper [[Alex Meret]], with goals coming from [[Leo Skiri Østigård|Leo Østigård]] and [[Piotr Zieliński]] for the hosts.<ref>{{cite web |title=Napoli-Real Madrid |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039104--napoli-vs-real-madrid/ |access-date=9 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=6 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240306000742/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039104--napoli-vs-real-madrid/ }}</ref> On matchday 3, Madrid defeated Braga away 2–1, with [[Rodrygo]] and Bellingham on the scoresheet, and [[Álvaro Djaló]] scoring for his team.<ref>{{cite web |title=Braga-Real Madrid |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039119/ |access-date=9 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=22 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230922005157/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039119/ }}</ref> On matchday 4, Madrid dominated Braga 3–0 at home with strikes from [[Brahim Díaz]], Vinícius, and Rodrygo.<ref>{{cite web |title=Real Madrid-Braga |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039144/ |access-date=9 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=22 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230922003656/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039144/ }}</ref> On matchday 5, Madrid obtained a 4–2 home win against Napoli, with goals coming from Vinícius, Bellingham, [[Nico Paz]], and [[Joselu]], as well as [[Giovanni Simeone]] and [[André-Frank Zambo Anguissa]] on the scoresheet for the visitors.<ref>{{cite web |title=Real Madrid-Napoli |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039158/ |access-date=9 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=22 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230922005155/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039158/ }}</ref> On matchday 6, they obtained a 3–2 win away against Union Berlin, after a Joselu brace and [[Dani Ceballos]] scoring the winner, with strikes from [[Kevin Volland]] and [[Alex Král]] for the hosts.<ref>{{cite web |title=Union Berlin-Real Madrid |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039167/ |access-date=9 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=22 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230922003654/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039167/ }}</ref>

In the round of 16, Madrid were drawn against German club [[RB Leipzig]]. In the first leg held at the [[Red Bull Arena (Leipzig)|Red Bull Arena]], a lone Brahim strike prevailed Madrid to a 1–0 away win.<ref>{{cite web |title=Leipzig-Real Madrid |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039914/ |access-date=9 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=14 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240214164412/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039914/ }}</ref> In the second leg, Madrid were held to a 1–1 draw, despite [[Willi Orbán]] scoring for his team, Vinícius's goal was enough for Madrid to advance 2–1 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite web |title=Real Madrid-Leipzig |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039924/ |access-date=9 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=18 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231218163322/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2039924/ }}</ref>

In the quarter-finals, Madrid were drawn against English champions and [[2022–23 UEFA Champions League|reigning UEFA Champions League winners]]
[[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] for the third consecutive season. The first leg at the Santiago Bernabéu ended in an intense 3–3 draw, in which goals from [[Bernardo Silva]], [[Phil Foden]], and [[Joško Gvardiol]] were denied by replies from a [[Rúben Dias]] own goal, Rodrygo, and [[Federico Valverde]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Real Madrid-Man City |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040494/ |access-date=9 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=15 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240315172851/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040494/ }}</ref> The second leg at the [[City of Manchester Stadium]] also ended in a draw, as Rodrygo's early goal was cancelled out by a [[Kevin De Bruyne]] equaliser. The match ended 1–1 after extra time and went into the penalty shootout to decide the winner after a 4–4 aggregate draw. It was Madrid's first Champions League shootout since the 2016 final. Goalkeeper [[Andriy Lunin]] saved two crucial penalties from Silva and [[Mateo Kovačić]], with only [[Luka Modrić]] missing his spot kick for the visitors, while [[Antonio Rüdiger]] scored the decisive final penalty to send his team to the semi-finals.<ref>{{cite web |title=Man City-Real Madrid |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040496/ |access-date=9 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=15 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240315172716/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040496/ }}</ref>

In the semi-finals, Madrid were drawn against German champions Bayern Munich, the first "[[Bayern Munich–Real Madrid rivalry|European Clásico]]" since the [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League]] season. In the first leg at the [[Allianz Arena]], a Vinícius double cancelled goals coming from [[Leroy Sané]] and [[Harry Kane]] for the hosts for a 2–2 draw.<ref>{{cite web |title=Bayern-Real Madrid |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040500/ |access-date=9 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=17 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240417222041/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040500/ }}</ref> In the second leg at the Santiago Bernabéu, with Madrid initially trailing from an [[Alphonso Davies]] goal, Joselu's double in the closing minutes of the game stunned the visitors to bring his team to a 2–1 victory in the match and 4–3 on aggregate, as they progressed to their sixth final in ten years.<ref>{{cite web |title=Real Madrid-Bayern |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040503/ |access-date=9 May 2024 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url-status=live |archive-date=17 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240417222044/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2040503/ }}</ref>

==Pre-match==
===Identity===
The visual identity of the 2024 UEFA Champions League final was unveiled at the group stage draw in Monaco on 31 August 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Brand identity unveiled for the 2024 UEFA Champions League final in London |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0284-18ddf64c1e93-e5c9c405914f-1000--brand-identity-unveiled-for-the-2024-uefa-champions-league-/ |website=UEFA.com|date=31 August 2023 |access-date=31 August 2023 |archive-date=31 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230831134220/https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/news/0284-18ddf64c1e93-e5c9c405914f-1000--brand-identity-unveiled-for-the-2024-uefa-champions-league-/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Officials===
[[File:Slavko Vincic.jpg|thumb|upright=.8|Slovenian referee [[Slavko Vinčić]] officiated the final.]]

On 13 May 2024, Slovenian referee [[Slavko Vinčić]] was appointed to take charge of the final by UEFA, along with fellow Slovenes Tomaž Klančnik and Andraž Kovačič as [[Assistant referee (association football)|assistant referees]], and Nejc Kajtazović as the [[video assistant referee]].<ref name="officials"/> The first three officials had previously officiated together in the [[2022 UEFA Europa League final]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0275-151e847d9f62-c7c70e5d01ff-1000/ |title=Referee teams appointed for 2022 UEFA club competition finals |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=11 May 2022 |access-date=14 May 2024 |archive-date=11 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220511175324/https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/news/0275-151e847d9f62-c7c70e5d01ff-1000/ |url-status=live}}</ref> They were joined by fellow countryman Rade Obrenović as assistant VAR and Frenchman [[François Letexier]] as the fourth official.<ref name="officials"/>

===Opening ceremony===
[[File:Lenny Kravitz 2018.jpg|thumb|[[Lenny Kravitz]], the headline act of the opening ceremony.]]
On 16 May 2024, American singer [[Lenny Kravitz]] was named as the headline act of the opening ceremony.<ref>{{cite news |title=Lenny Kravitz to headline UEFA Champions League Final Kick Off Show by Pepsi |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/028d-1ae6b6682ef6-eefb8c68a8da-1000--lenny-kravitz-to-headline-uefa-champions-league-final-ki/ |website=UEFA.com|date=17 May 2024 |access-date=17 May 2024 |archive-date=17 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240517052540/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/028d-1ae6b6682ef6-eefb8c68a8da-1000--lenny-kravitz-to-headline-uefa-champions-league-final-ki/ |url-status=live}}</ref> He performed with his band a medley of his songs: "[[Fly Away (Lenny Kravitz song)|Fly Away]]", "[[Human (Lenny Kravitz song)|Human]]" and "[[Are You Gonna Go My Way (song)|Are You Gonna Go My Way]]".<ref>{{cite web |url=https://esto.com.mx/821831-champions-league-2024-lenny-kravitz-se-robo-el-show-en-el-borussia-dortmund-vs-real-madrid/ |title=¡Brutal! Así fue el show de Lenny Kravitz en la final de la Champions League 2024 |trans-title=Brutal! This was Lenny Kravitz's show in the 2024 Champions League final |work=Esto.com.mx |date=1 June 2024 |access-date=2 June 2024 |language=es |archive-date=2 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240602013408/https://esto.com.mx/821831-champions-league-2024-lenny-kravitz-se-robo-el-show-en-el-borussia-dortmund-vs-real-madrid/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
[[File:FC RB Salzburg v.Real Madrid (Testspiel, 7. August 2019) 29.jpg|thumb|upright=.8|[[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] defender [[Dani Carvajal]] was named [[Player of the match#Association football|man of the match]] and became the first defender to score in a final since [[Sergio Ramos]] in [[2016 UEFA Champions League final|2016]].]]

The match kicked off at 20:00 local time with an attendance of 86,212 fans. In the 21st minute, [[Mats Hummels]] played a pass to [[Karim Adeyemi]], who rounded [[Thibaut Courtois]] by nudging the ball to the goalkeeper's right and attempting to shoot, but [[Dani Carvajal]] tracked back to block his effort. Two minutes later, Adeyemi played a pass through the centre of the Madrid defence and released [[Niclas Füllkrug]], who struck the woodwork. Dortmund then produced a counter-attack after Brandt played a pass to Adeyemi, who was unable to beat Courtois from a tight angle, with the latter stretching out to make the initial parry, before saving Füllkrug's follow-up attempt. There were four minutes of added time after a series of [[Pitch invasion#Association football|pitch invasions]] earlier in the match.<ref name="SportingNews">{{cite web |last=Cox |first=Brad |url=https://www.sportingnews.com/us/champions-league/news/champions-league-final-2024-live-score-result-real-madrid-dortmund/0a034fd36e18781a619e15f1 |title=Champions League final score: Dortmund vs. Real Madrid result, highlights as Vinicius seals 2024 UCL title |work=Sporting News |date=2 June 2024|access-date=2 June 2024|archive-date=2 June 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240602015436/https://www.sportingnews.com/us/champions-league/news/champions-league-final-2024-live-score-result-real-madrid-dortmund/0a034fd36e18781a619e15f1|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="TheGuardian">{{cite web |last=Murray |first=Scott |url=https://www.sportingnews.com/us/champions-league/news/champions-league-final-2024-live-score-result-real-madrid-dortmund/0a034fd36e18781a619e15f1 |title=Borussia Dortmund 0–2 Real Madrid: Champions League final 2024 – as it happened |website=The Guardian |date=1 June 2024|access-date=2 June 2024|archive-date=2 June 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240602015436/https://www.sportingnews.com/us/champions-league/news/champions-league-final-2024-live-score-result-real-madrid-dortmund/0a034fd36e18781a619e15f1|url-status=live}}</ref>

Neither side made any lineup changes during the interval. In the 49th minute, [[Toni Kroos]] whipped in a free kick towards the Dortmund penalty area, with Carvajal sending his header over the bar. Madrid continued their pressure as [[Vinícius Júnior]] whipped a cross towards the penalty area, which [[Nico Schlotterbeck]] nodded straight to Carvajal, whose half-volley effort was blocked by [[Ian Maatsen]]. Dortmund pushed again for a goal as Adeyemi played a cross into the penalty area towards Füllkrug, who managed to direct a powerful header that was saved by Courtois. Vinícius played another cross from the left flank to former Dortmund midfielder [[Jude Bellingham]], who missed the ball which flew inches wide of the post. Dortmund then made their first substitution of the final, as Adeyemi came off for [[Marco Reus]].<ref name="SportingNews"/><ref name="TheGuardian"/>

With 15 minutes to go, Carvajal gave Madrid the lead in the 74th minute, heading in Kroos' corner from the left. Madrid had another chance to score, with [[Eduardo Camavinga]] cutting the ball back from the byline on the left to Bellingham, who looked to strike the ball past [[Gregor Kobel]], only for Schlotterbeck to block his effort. Dortmund then made two attacking changes, as captain [[Emre Can]] and [[Julian Brandt]] came off for [[Donyell Malen]] and [[Sébastien Haller]]. Madrid came close to scoring a second again, as Kobel saved Camavinga's effort from outside the box and [[Nacho (footballer, born 1990)|Nacho]]'s header from a Kroos corner.<ref name="SportingNews"/><ref name="TheGuardian"/>

In the 83rd minute, Maatsen played a pass that was intercepted by Bellingham, who teed up Vinícius to his left, with the latter taking a touch and putting the ball past Kobel to double Madrid's lead. Madrid then made their first changes as Bellingham and Kroos came off for [[Joselu]] and [[Luka Modrić]]. Dortmund made their last substitution, with [[Jadon Sancho]] brought off for [[Jamie Bynoe-Gittens]]. Dortmund thought they had pulled one back, as Malen played a cross into the penalty area to Füllkrug, who powered a header into the left-hand side of the net, leaving Courtois with no chance. However, the goal was immediately disallowed by the referee after Füllkrug was shown to be in an offside position. Madrid made their two other substitutions in stoppage time, with [[Rodrygo]] and Vinícius brought off for [[Éder Militão]] and [[Lucas Vázquez]]. The match ended after five minutes of stoppage time, with Madrid winning 2–0.<ref name="SportingNews"/><ref name="TheGuardian"/>

