==Post-match==
[[File:Carlo Ancelotti 2016 (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright=.8|[[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]]'s [[Carlo Ancelotti]] won his fifth Champions League title as a manager and seventh overall.]] 

With the win, Real Madrid secured their record-extending 15th European Cup/Champions League title, and their sixth in the previous eleven seasons. It maintained Madrid's perfect final record in the Champions League era with nine wins, having last lost the final in 1981.<ref name="RSSSF"/> Madrid won the Champions League [[European Cup and UEFA Champions League records and statistics#Unbeaten sides|unbeaten]] for the first time, with nine wins and four draws.<ref name="summary"/> They also completed their fifth [[Double (association football)|European double]] (after [[1956–57 Real Madrid CF season|1956–57]], [[1957–58 Real Madrid CF season|1957–58]], [[2016–17 Real Madrid CF season|2016–17]] and [[2021–22 Real Madrid CF season|2021–22]]), having won the [[2023–24 La Liga]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.thescore.com/news/2921332 |title=Ancelotti: Real Madrid will be back with same winning 'desire' |work=[[theScore]] |agency=[[Agence France-Presse]] |date=1 June 2024 |access-date=2 June 2024 |archive-date=5 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240605073353/https://www.thescore.com/news/2921332 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Four Madrid players (Dani Carvajal, Toni Kroos, Luka Modrić, and Nacho) equalled [[Paco Gento]]'s record of winning six European Cup/Champions League titles; all but Kroos have won all six of their titles with Real.<ref>{{cite web |last=Benge |first=James |url=https://www.cbssports.com/soccer/news/real-madrids-ucl-hero-dani-carvajal-eyes-passing-legendary-paco-gento-why-not-dream-of-getting-another/ |title=Real Madrid's UCL hero Dani Carvajal eyes passing legendary Paco Gento: 'Why not dream of getting another?' |work=[[CBS Sports]] |date=1 June 2024 |access-date=2 June 2024 |archive-date=2 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240602010952/https://www.cbssports.com/soccer/news/real-madrids-ucl-hero-dani-carvajal-eyes-passing-legendary-paco-gento-why-not-dream-of-getting-another/ |url-status=live}}</ref> In addition, Carvajal joined Gento as the only players to start in six different winning finals in the competition. Madrid manager Carlo Ancelotti extended his record as the [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League winning managers|most successful manager]] in the competition's history, with his fifth title, and his third in charge of ''Los Blancos''.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0252-0e98c715bb79-dea77f56e2bd-1000/ |title=Champions League final records and statistics |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=1 June 2024 |access-date=2 June 2024 |archive-date=5 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240605073408/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0252-0e98c715bb79-dea77f56e2bd-1000/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

Meanwhile, Borussia Dortmund manager [[Edin Terzić]] stepped down from his role on 13 June 2024, nearly two weeks after losing the final. He stated that after the final, he asked for a meeting with the club's senior management team because he felt that "the club's new era should begin with a new man on the touchline", and wished Dortmund the very best.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.bvb.de/eng/News/Overview/Edin-Terzic-leaves-Borussia-Dortmund |title=Edin Terzic leaves Borussia Dortmund |publisher=Borussia Dortmund |date=13 June 2024 |access-date=11 January 2025 |archive-date=13 June 2024 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240613164447/http://www.bvb.de/eng/News/Overview/Edin-Terzic-leaves-Borussia-Dortmund |url-status=dead}}</ref> The next day, Terzić's assistant and former Dortmund player [[Nuri Şahin]] was appointed as head coach on a three-year contract.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bvb.de/eng/News/Overview/Sahin-appointed-as-BVB-head-coach |title=Sahin appointed as BVB head coach |publisher=Borussia Dortmund |date=14 June 2024 |access-date=11 January 2025 |archive-date=14 June 2024 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240614151308/https://www.bvb.de/eng/News/Overview/Sahin-appointed-as-BVB-head-coach |url-status=dead}}</ref>

=== Crime===
After the game, the [[Metropolitan Police]] said that over 2,000 officers had been on duty for the game. 53 arrests had been made, mainly for trying to enter the stadium without a ticket and five arrests for entering the field of play. The game had only just kicked off when pitch invaders approached a number of the players.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Jones |first1=Simon |last2=Jackson |first2=Liz |title=Champions League: Police arrest 53 around Wembley final |website=BBC News |date=1 June 2024 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cpdd27jem92o |access-date=2 June 2024 |archive-date=1 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240601232350/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cpdd27jem92o |url-status=live}}</ref> 

Three people were later charged with entering the field of play contrary to Section 4 of the [[Football (Offences) Act 1991]]. Their court appearances were arranged for June and July 2024. The police said that most attempts to gain access to the ground without a ticket had been thwarted following an investment of £5&nbsp;million, on new security lanes, improved fencing, additional stewards and ticket checks and their increased enforcement of the [[public spaces protection order]], carried out after the mass unrest at the Euro 2020 final in July 2021.<ref>{{cite web |last=Howard |first=Jacqueline |title=Three people charged after Champions League pitch invasion |website=BBC News |date=3 June 2024 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c9eemvpe52lo |access-date=3 June 2024 |archive-date=3 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240603021109/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c9eemvpe52lo |url-status=live}}</ref> A Russian vlogger, Andrey Burim, was later reported to have offered 30&nbsp;million [[ruble]]s to anyone who entered the pitch with his handle on their t-shirts.<ref>{{cite web |last=Neal |first=Will |title=Russian vlogger 'unmasked as instigator behind Champions League chaos' |website=Metro |date=2 June 2024 |url=https://metro.co.uk/2024/06/02/50-fans-arrested-champions-league-final-chaos-20955456/ |access-date=3 June 2024 |archive-date=3 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240603013334/https://metro.co.uk/2024/06/02/50-fans-arrested-champions-league-final-chaos-20955456/ |url-status=live}}</ref>
On 3 June, a Ukrainian man pled guilty at [[Westminster Magistrates' Court]] to entering the field of play and admitted he was inspired by Andrey Burim. He was fined £1,000, which was cut to £660 following his guilty plea. He was also ordered to pay £85 costs and a £264 witness surcharge.<ref>{{cite web |last=Warren |first=Jess |title=Champions League final: Man handed UK ban over pitch invasion bid |website=BBC News |date=3 June 2024 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c722mnv9x2mo |access-date=4 June 2024 |archive-date=4 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240604002408/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c722mnv9x2mo |url-status=live}}</ref>

==Broadcasting==
In Spain, the final was broadcast by [[Televisión Española]] (TVE), with an average of 6.1&nbsp;million viewers (48.8% of share) and a peak of 7.3&nbsp;million (52.5% of share) at 22:44 [[Central European Summer Time|CEST]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=RTVE |first=PRENSA |date=2 June 2024 |title=El Madrid conquista su 15ª Champions ante más de 6,1 millones en La 1 (48,8%), lo más visto de la temporada |url=https://www.rtve.es/rtve/20240602/madrid-conquista-15a-champions-millones-mas-visto-temporada-4-1-48-8-la-1/16129602.shtml |access-date=2 June 2024 |website=RTVE.es |language=es |archive-date=2 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240602091753/https://www.rtve.es/rtve/20240602/madrid-conquista-15a-champions-millones-mas-visto-temporada-4-1-48-8-la-1/16129602.shtml |url-status=live}}</ref> In total, at least 10.4&nbsp;million unique viewers watched the match at some point.<ref name=":1" /> In addition, public streaming service [[RTVE Play]] gathered almost 800,000 unique viewers, with a total of 1.5&nbsp;million views.<ref name=":1" /> Post-match celebrations were followed by 3.2&nbsp;million people.<ref name=":1" /> Likewise, the [[Movistar Plus+]] subscription platform broadcast the match, gathering 655,000 viewers.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Romero |first=Gabriel Arias |date=2 June 2024 |title=TVE (48.8%) arrasa con la final de la Champions y la victoria del Real Madrid ante más de 6 millones de espectadores |url=https://www.eldiario.es/vertele/audiencias-tv/sabado-1-junio-2024-final-champions-league-real-madrid-tve-la1-millones-espectadores_1_11415534.html |access-date=2 June 2024 |website=Vertele |language=es |archive-date=2 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240602090954/https://www.eldiario.es/vertele/audiencias-tv/sabado-1-junio-2024-final-champions-league-real-madrid-tve-la1-millones-espectadores_1_11415534.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

